I am a longtime student of George Gilder and have always believed in free enterprise. But we have to grapple with the fact that: 

(1) We do not have free markets now, and "free markets" are always based on a negotiated set of rules, not a set of abstract principles;
(2) There is no viable path for reducing the size of the welfare state, because voters do not want to do so;
(3) There are downsides to extreme financialization and concentration of the economy;
(4) Some protectionism for military and industry is necessary for national security; 
(5) Most Americans do not believe that Republican economic policies will make their lives better; 
(6) Young people are feeling increasingly disenfranchised from the economy.

It's not enough to say "free markets." We have to show normal voters how we can get inflation under control, how free markets will directly benefit their families, and how we will ensure that young people feel a sense of stakes and opportunity in the economy.

